% ============================================================
% Task 5: Multi-hop Compound Reasoning
% Requires deduction, induction, AND abduction in sequence.
% Designed to stress resource limits maximally.
%
% Scenario: Reasoning about animal taxonomy and behaviour
% under full NAL inference modes
% ============================================================

% --- Deductive chain component ---
<animal --> livingThing>. %{1.00 0.90}
<mammal --> animal>. %{1.00 0.90}
<dog --> mammal>. %{1.00 0.90}

% --- Inductive component ---
<fido --> dog>. %{1.00 0.90}
<fido --> canBark>. %{1.00 0.90}
<rex --> dog>. %{1.00 0.90}
<rex --> canBark>. %{1.00 0.90}

% --- Abductive component ---
% Rule: barking implies a dog is nearby
<barking --> dogNearby>. %{0.90 0.85}
% Observation: there is barking
<event --> barking>. %{1.00 0.90}

% --- Additional linking facts ---
<livingThing --> hasMetabolism>. %{1.00 0.85}
<dog --> canBark>. %{0.90 0.80}

% Multi-hop query: Is the observed event related to a living thing nearby?
<event --> dogNearby>?
<dog --> livingThing>?

% Expected: Requires combining:
%   dog-->mammal-->animal-->livingThing (deduction, 3-hop)
%   fido/rex canBark => dog-->canBark (induction)
%   barking=>dogNearby + event-->barking => event-->dogNearby (deduction+abduction)
% Ground-truth: f* ~ 0.65, c* ~ 0.35 (most demanding task)
